Let $w = w_1 w_2 \cdots w_n$ be a finite word of length $n$ over the binary alphabet $\{0,1\}$. A run (also called a maximal repetition) in $w$ is a triple $(i, j, p)$ with $1 \le i \le j \le n$ and a positive integer $p$ such that:
(i) the factor $w_i w_{i+1} \cdots w_j$ has period $p$ (meaning $w_k = w_{k+p}$ for all $i \le k \le j-p$);
(ii) $2p \le j - i + 1$ (the factor has exponent at least $2$);
(iii) the period and the occurrence are maximal, i.e. neither $w_{i-1} w_i \cdots w_j$ (if $i > 1$) nor $w_i \cdots w_{j+1}$ (if $j < n$) has period $p$.

Let $\rho(w)$ denote the number of runs in $w$, and let $\rho(n) = \max\{\rho(w) : w \in \{0,1\}^n\}$.

Prove or disprove: $\rho(n) < n$ for every positive integer $n$.

